Add a bounded 16 KiB recent-log ring + GET /api/logs to the Wi-Fi
variants' local trusted-LAN HTTP server. Applied only to
nanoc6-wifi and atoms3-lite-wifi via variants.json;
nanoc6-thread never applies, compiles, links, or starts any of it.

Scope, keep minimal (no log viewer UI, no OTA, no WebSocket, no
authentication, no settings changes):

  * MODIFY main/aliro_local_web.h: declare
      esp_err_t AliroLocalWebLogInit(void);
    which app_main calls exactly once at boot to install the log
    sink. The header prose reflects the implementation: the ring
    lock is a statically initialised portMUX with no runtime
    construction, so this entry point always returns ESP_OK.

  * MODIFY main/aliro_local_web.cpp: add a static 16 KiB ring
    (s_log_ring), a portMUX spinlock (s_log_lock,
    portMUX_INITIALIZER_UNLOCKED — statically initialised), a
    kLogLineMax = 256 stack format buffer, and:
      - log_sink_vprintf: reentrant, formats OUTSIDE the ring lock
        with independent va_copy values, invokes the captured
        prior serial vprintf and returns ITS return value so
        callers keep the same byte-count contract, then enters a
        bounded critical section (portENTER/EXIT_CRITICAL_SAFE)
        to copy the formatted line into the ring. The critical
        section replaces a no-wait mutex on purpose: the reviewer
        requires that no diagnostic data is silently dropped. If
        vsnprintf reports truncation, the tail of the stack
        buffer is rewritten with a clear
        '...[log truncated]\n' marker before it is copied to the
        ring, so a reader of the snapshot sees the loss instead
        of a silently cropped message. The sink NEVER calls
        ESP_LOG and NEVER allocates.
      - log_ring_snapshot: allocates a kLogRingSize heap buffer
        BEFORE entering the critical section, memcpy's the ring
        chronologically (head..end + 0..head when wrapped) UNDER
        the critical section, releases the critical section
        BEFORE returning to the caller, and hands the caller
        ownership of the heap buffer. Handles the allocation
        failure explicitly.
      - logs_get_handler: calls log_ring_snapshot, sets
        Content-Type text/plain; charset=utf-8 and Cache-Control:
        no-store, sends the buffer with httpd_resp_send, then
        free()s the buffer. An out-of-memory snapshot is surfaced
        as 503 with a small JSON error body; any other failure is
        500.
      - AliroLocalWebLogInit: idempotent installer for the log
        sink; the ring lock is a portMUX so it needs no runtime
        construction.
    httpd_config max_uri_handlers is bumped from 6 to 7; the new
    register call rides the same atomic URI-registration failure
    branch that already retains s_server when httpd_stop also
    fails. No log text is redacted anywhere.

  * MODIFY main/app_main.cpp: call AliroLocalWebLogInit() as the
    very first line inside app_main() so the ring captures the
    earliest boot log lines.

Explicit non-goals for this patch: no log viewer UI, no OTA, no
WebSocket, no authentication, no settings backend changes.

